terminal-system — AI-assisted two-panel terminal
=================================================

Usage: terminal-system [claude|codex]

Opens a tmux session with two panels:

  - Left panel: a normal shell. This is where commands actually run.
  - Right panel: type instructions in natural language, e.g.
    "create user Pippo". The chosen AI engine translates the
    instruction into shell command(s), which are typed into the left
    panel in real time.

Right-panel commands:
  :cmd <command>          type a shell command manually, without AI
  :manual                 prompt for a manual shell command
  :auto readonly|off      toggle automatic execution (default: off)
  :context on|off|show|clear   opt-in AI context from left-panel output
  :engine claude|codex    switch AI engine for the rest of the session
  :quit                   end the session

Engine selection:
  Start with "terminal-system codex" or "terminal-system claude" to
  choose an engine explicitly. If the active engine is installed but
  fails at runtime, for example because its quota is exhausted,
  terminal-system tries the other available engine and switches to it
  when the fallback call succeeds.

Confirmation:
  By default every generated or manual command waits for you to press
  Enter (or type "e" to edit it, or "n" to cancel it) before it runs.
  With ":auto readonly", a command runs without confirmation only if
  its first word is a known read-only program (ls, cat, grep, ps, df,
  du, git status/log/diff, systemctl status, journalctl, dpkg -l, apt
  list/search/show, and similar) AND it contains none of the shell
  metacharacters that could turn a read-only command into something
  else (redirects, pipes, backticks, $(), ;, &). A fixed set of
  always-dangerous patterns (rm -rf /, mkfs, dd onto /dev, fork bombs,
  any recursive chmod on /, shutdown/reboot, killall -9,
  userdel/groupdel on root/sudo/admin, iptables -F, writes onto common
  block devices under /dev) is checked as a second layer. Anything not
  explicitly recognized as safe always stops for confirmation, even in
  auto-mode.
  When a command is likely to ask for input, such as sudo, ssh, passwd,
  an editor, or a pager, focus moves to the left panel so passwords and
  other interactive prompts are typed into the shell running the
  command, not into the instruction panel. When the command finishes and
  the left panel returns to the shell, focus moves back to the
  instruction panel. Non-interactive commands keep focus on the
  instruction panel so you can keep asking questions.

Session memory:
  Off by default. With ":context on", instructions, generated commands,
  and recent left-panel output are kept as context for the rest of the
  session (so "now add him to the sudo group" can refer back to a user
  you just created); output is scanned for common secret patterns
  (passwords, tokens, API keys, and similar) before being stored, on a
  best-effort basis. Nothing is written to disk either way — history
  disappears when the session ends, and ":context clear" empties it
  sooner. ":context show" prints what's currently stored.

Isolation:
  terminal-system runs on its own dedicated tmux server (a named socket
  via tmux -L), separate from any other tmux session you have open, so
  its mouse/copy-paste key bindings don't leak into your other sessions.

Requires:
  The claude and/or codex CLI already installed and authenticated.
  Neither is a hard package dependency.
